In article <1991Mar8.232011.3539@news.cs.brandeis.edu> cos@chaos.cs.brandeis.edu (Ofer Inbar) writes:
>I think spells should wear out.  It comes from precedent set by many
>role-playing games, and makes some sort of 'sense'.  I remember
I think spells are the only thing that needs a change (other changes are
welcome of course). I don't know how but it would be fun when you really can
fight with magic. Isn't it a little bit strange that a wizard has no magic
to protect himself against a normal weapon? I can't imagine that Gandalf was
very strong person dressed as a knight. How a magic fight should look like is
difficult to say because there is no good example in the literature. And even
D&D can't help because a wizard alone can't survive.

Maybe there should be 3 kinds of spells.

Toolspells like secret door detection, light, knocking, wizard lock and
digging.

Protectionspells. Casting such spell takes no move (like stethoscope) and
protects against the next attack relating to that spell. How much you are
protected depends on magic power, intelligence etc.

And of course attackingspells. The damage they do should also depend on
magic power etc.

Spells should not wear out and therefor a spell should not disappear after you
have read it (maybe when you become more intelligent you can read it again).

Also wearing or wielding metal should have a very bad effect on your magic.

And what about a wand of anti-magic (instead of a wand of light or secret door
detection)?

This are just suggestions because I never read spellbooks because they killed
me often and I can't remember they were ever really helpfull.

